<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T6.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">Table 6</span>: </span><span id="S4.T6.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">Ablation study on different uses of context memory for generating EgoGS step captions. “Frames Hier.” refers to the hierarchical-aware frame sampling strategy, while “Previous Output” refers to using previous predictions as auxiliary information. InternVL2-76B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> is used across all the experiments.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12145v1/figure4_2.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:685/730;" width="685" height="730" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.F5.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Qualitative results. For the GT substep caption <span id="S4.F5.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">”Place broccoli strainer in pot”</span>, OpenHOUSE provides <span id="S4.F5.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">”The person is placing the steamer basket with the rinsed broccoli onto the stove.”</span>, which aligns better with the actual visual content.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prediction Delay</span>
To quantitatively assess the promptness of OpenHOUSE, we compare its prediction delay with SDVC using the AEDT metric from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, which measures the time gap between when a true positive prediction is generated and when it should ideally be produced.
Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.2 Results and Analysis ‣ 4 Experiment and Analysis ‣ Open-ended Hierarchical Streaming Video Understanding with Vision Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> presents the results, showing that SDVC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite> introduces significant delays compared to our strictly online approach.
Moreover, simply shortening the interval between decoding points significantly degrades localization performance, as noted in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>, making it unsuitable for online approaches.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Utilization of Context Memory</span>
As discussed in Section <a href="#S3.SS3.SSS2" title="3.3.2 VLM ‣ 3.3 Approach: OpenHOUSE ‣ 3 Target Task and Our Approach ‣ Open-ended Hierarchical Streaming Video Understanding with Vision Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3.2</span></a>, we leverage context memory during VLM inference.
The key information in the context memory includes hierarchical frame membership (e.g., used when selecting frames within the substeps of the current step for VLM inference) and previous text-based predictions.
Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.2 Results and Analysis ‣ 4 Experiment and Analysis ‣ Open-ended Hierarchical Streaming Video Understanding with Vision Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> presents the results of these experiments, showing that incorporating context memory that leverages the hierarchical structure improves performance while requiring only 40% of the frames.
This reduction in frame usage significantly lowers the computational complexity of VLM inference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inference speed</span>
We evaluated the inference efficiency on a 46-minute video at 16 fps (totaling 2758 * 16 frames) using four RTX 3090 GPUs with the VLM model InternVL2-40B-AWQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. OpenHOUSE achieves an average processing speed of 24 fps, which is <span id="S4.SS2.p11.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">16 times faster</span> compared to using the VLM for every frame in the same environment. This shows that our framework significantly improves the efficiency of VLM utilization given the same input.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qualitative results</span>
Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.2 Results and Analysis ‣ 4 Experiment and Analysis ‣ Open-ended Hierarchical Streaming Video Understanding with Vision Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows the qualitative results of our OpenHOUSE framework.
The video is processed in an online manner, generating both substep and step captions.
We observed that the generated captions exhibit high semantic similarity to the ground truth (GT) captions, providing even more detailed descriptions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Additional experiments</span> Experiments on various VLMs, description generation for incomplete action instances, additional details on the mentioned experiments, and demo videos are provided in the supplementary materials.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With advancing VLMs, we see their integration into streaming settings as the next step for online action perception.
In this context, OpenHOUSE is a key step, laying the foundation for future research.</p>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<br class="ltx_break ltx_centering">
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:144%;">Supplementary Material 
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Details of Metrics</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section provides detailed explanations of the evaluation metrics presented in the main text.</p>
</div>
